A||||11||Alex2004||What's the easiest engine swap for an 84?||||1||
Z||000000||Alex2004||12-25-2000||08:00 PM||||My dad and I both want more power than the 4 cyl. And i was just wondering for anyone who has done it, what is the easiest engine swap since it's an 84? Please let me know. Thanks<P>Alex...||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||12-25-2000||08:09 PM||||I'm going to say another 4 cyl. However, I assume you want a 6cyl, so you would need a 6cyl donor car to get all the extras off of it (wiring, mounts, etc).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex2004||12-25-2000||08:14 PM||||I guess i should have been more specific. I would want a V6 but without having to re-wire alot of things. would a 3.4 be easier than a 2.8?||||reg||3
Z||000003||KRMFiero||12-25-2000||08:24 PM||||The 2.8 and the 3.4 are basically the same engine....  just so you know i am putting a Caddy engine in my 84  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    (how long it will take me/how hard it will be is unknow)<P><BR>heheh hope i helped<P>kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||12-25-2000||09:27 PM||||seems to be what i keep hearing. if you have the 4cyl. pretty much every thing is hard. i'll probally put in whatever i can get my hands on if the deals good enough, but go with a 3.4 if i give up and try to go with the most stock route.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex2004||12-25-2000||09:40 PM||||Well thanks, i was asking my dad about it just now and he was basically saying the same thing. I want to put a Caddy engine in mine too...haha...i'm only 15, on my 16 b-day i would probably kill my self if i had the caddy engine in mine...LOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">Well thanks though, i guess we will try to modify the 4 cyl or something. Thanks again!||||reg||7
Z||000006||JohnnyK||12-25-2000||11:01 PM||||Well, I don't think the 2.8 or the 3.1 is too hard. The 3.4 you have to relocate the starter, but with the 2.8 and 3.1, just get some parts that you need off a junker (someone help me with what those parts are) and have at her. I don't think the wiring would be too hard.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||12-26-2000||04:07 AM||||Easiest for an 84?  Like the others said, another iron duke.  It sucks, but the wiring in the 84's are completly different than the other years, plus the cradle's don't have some mounting points for the v6.  <P>If you don't want to kill yourself with speed, the best thing to do would be to find another duke and rebuild it, throw in a cam, massage it a bit, etc.. won't be crazy fast but it should still be plenty fun.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Motorhead||12-26-2000||01:29 PM||||My "ALL TIME FAVORITE SWAP"!!!<BR>I think this pic came from Archies but not sure. Whoever accomplished this miracle, I LOVE YOU.<BR> "The Stuff Wet Dreams Are Made Of"!<P>------------------<BR> bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10592337&p=36239095"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||12-26-2000||09:22 PM||||If you do a V6 find a V6 cradle from 85-87 and use that. <P>The "easiest" V6 would be to pull the entire harness from a V6 car and put it in the 84. That would solve a whole bunch of problems. It all goes together with a bunch of big plugs. It more time consuming that dificult if you lable things as you pull it apart.<P>Preferably the donor is an 87 which also gives you the gen2 headlight lift system and wiring for high mount brake light.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-26-2000).]||||reg||1
